{{smaller|the optic chiasm.—'''Area septalis,''' in ''anat.'', the septal area; the mesal surface of each half of the septum lucidum.—'''Areas of Cohnheim,''' in ''anat.'', the polygonal areas into which the cross-section of a muscle-fiber is seen to be divided. Also called ''fields of Cohnheim.''—'''Area vasculosa,''' in ''embryol.'', the vascular area about the clear space, when blood-vessels are developed in the mesoblast.—'''Area vitellina,''' in ''embryol.'', the yolk-area beyond the vascular area in meroblastic eggs.—'''Blind area, faunal area,''' etc. See the adjectives.}}

'''areach†''', ''v.'' [Early mod. E. also ''areche, arreach, aretch'' (pret. ''araught, arraught''), Sc. ''areik'', etc., < ME. ''arechen'', < AS. ''ārǣcan'' (= OHG. ''arreichōn'', MHG. G. ''erreichen''), reach, reach to, get at, < ''ā-'' + ''rǣcan'', reach: see ''a-''<sup>1</sup> and ''reach.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To reach; get at; get; obtain.—'''2.''' To reach, hand, or deliver (a thing to a person).
{{smaller block|To whom Y schal ''areche'' a sop of breed.
{{right|''Wyclif'', John xiii. 26.}}}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To reach; stretch; extend.

'''aread†, arede†''' (a-rēd′), ''v. t.'' [In mod. use archaic, and of unsettled orthography; also written ''areed, arreed;'' < ME. ''areden'', < AS. ''ārǣdan'', ''ārēdan'' (weak verb, pret. ''ārǣdde'', pp. ''ārǣded'', ''ārǣd'', but orig. strong), determine, decree, explain, interpret, read (= OHG. ''arrātan'', MHG. ''erraten'', G. ''errathen'', guess, conjecture), < ''ā-'' + ''rǣdan'', determine, counsel, read: see ''a-''<sup>1</sup> and ''read, rede.''] '''1.''' To declare; tell; interpret; explain.
{{smaller block|''Arede'' my dremes. ''Chaucer'', Death of Blanche, l. 289.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Arede'', good gentle swaine,
If in the dale below, or on yon plaine,
Or is the village situate in a grove.</poem>
{{right|''W. Browne'', Britannia’s Pastorals, i. 3.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|''Areed'' my counsel aright, and I will warrant thee for the nonce.
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, I. 136.}}}}
'''2.''' To counsel; advise; direct; teach.
{{smaller block|But mark what I ''arreed'' thee now; Avaunt.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 962.}}}}
'''3.''' To guess; conjecture.
{{smaller block|<poem>So hard this Idole was to be ''ared'',
That Florimell herselfe in all mens vew
She seem’d to passe. {{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. v. 15.}}</poem>}}
'''4.''' To read.
{{smaller block|Her hardly open’d book, which to ''aread'' is easie.
{{right|''John Hall'', Poems, p. 61.}}}}

'''aread†''', '''arede†''', ''n.'' [< ''aread'', ''v.''] Advice; discourse; narration.
{{smaller block|<poem>Fayre ''areedes''
Of tydinges straunge. {{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. ix. 28.}}</poem>}}

'''areæ''', ''n.'' Plural of ''area'' in its anatomical senses.

'''areal''' (ā′rē-al), ''a.'' [< L. ''arealis'', of a threshing-floor (cf. ML. ''arealis'', ''areale'', n., an area), < ''area'', an open space, threshing-floor, etc.: see ''area''.] Pertaining to or of the nature of an area: as, ''areal'' interstices.
{{smaller block|The rapid study of the ''areal'' geology of the country, including the outlines of its commercial problems.
{{right|''Science'', IV. 362.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Areal coördinates.''' See ''coördinate''.}}

'''areality''' (ā-rē-al′i̤-ti), ''n.'' [< ''areal'' + ''-ity''.] The condition or relation of anything in respect to area. ''N. E. D.'' [Rare.]

